 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on in the body",
   "text": "CLN3 disease happens because of changes in a gene (a set of instructions in our cells) called CLN3 [E2eacf58, E30dc69f]. This gene normally makes a protein that sits in the outer wall of tiny cell parts called lysosomes, which act like the cell's cleanup crew [Eaaac865]. When the CLN3 protein is missing or not working, a fatty, waxy material called ceroid lipofuscin builds up inside cells [Eb0132b8, E633c826]. This build-up is linked to a slow loss of nerve cells, especially in the brain [Ed7956f4, E4a5b1d9]. The condition can also affect the heart, with changes in heart rhythm and the heart muscle starting around the mid-teen years [E1f2dcff]. Specific harmful changes in the CLN3 gene, including ones named p.Trp275Ter and p.Leu28fs, have been identified as causing this disease [E7413b5d, E8daea89].",
   "citations": [
    "E2eacf58",
    "E30dc69f",
    "Eaaac865",
    "Eb0132b8",
    "E633c826",
    "Ed7956f4",
    "E4a5b1d9",
