   ],
   "status": "ok"
  },
  {
   "key": "similar",
   "title": "How it compares to other conditions",
   "text": "A condition called CLN2 disease is listed as a lookalike for CLN3, meaning it can look similar but comes from a different cause [E4b46978]. CLN2 is caused by a problem with a different protein, an enzyme called TPP1, rather than the CLN3 gene [E4b46978]. Even so, children with CLN2 can have sudden muscle jerks called myoclonus, the same as children with CLN3 [E0282bf7, E7982300]. Both conditions can involve a gradual loss of vision over time [Ece5a0d1, E24d0838]. Both can also show no detectable signal on an eye test called an electroretinogram [E505ff82, E692fe55].",
   "citations": [
    "E4b46978",
    "E0282bf7",
    "E7982300",
