   "status": "ok"
  },
  {
   "key": "what_exists",
   "title": "What support and research already exists",
   "text": "There is a patient group focused on CLN3 called Beyond Batten Disease Foundation, based in the US, which works to raise awareness and funds for research [Eda2ebc2]. Another group, The Batten Disease Support & Research Association, keeps up-to-date information on every known form of Batten disease [E0294533]. The NIH's NINDS funds research grants about CLN3, including one on a possible antisense oligonucleotide therapy and one looking at a gene called TTYH3 [Ed725662, E0685c9c, Ea22db1a]. The NIH's NICHD also funds a research project studying CLN3 [Eba9be50, E2a71a2d]. Several studies are recruiting in the United States, including one looking at an infused cell treatment given through the spine [Eb47ea6a, E55ebc77, Ec109198], one called Investigations of Juvenile Neuronal Ceroid Lipofuscinosis [E19ee7ea, Ef32c051], and one studying family caregiving [E83e587a, E35ecde9]. A trial of a medicine called PLX-200 for CLN3 was later withdrawn [Ec50667b, E489962f, Ef038fca]. Researchers including Erika Augustine, Jill M Weimer, and Susan Lynn Cotman are listed as working on grants that study CLN3 [Ec74b030, E2a71a2d, E6d64cb3, Eb38c2d1, Edd559f3].",
